 ## Overview
-Primitive Geometry and Distribution defines the mathematical shape, parameterization, and spatial distribution of the basis functions used to represent a 3D scene in Gaussian Splatting. It solves the problem of how to discretize continuous radiance fields into a set of learnable, differentiable volumetric or surface elements that can be efficiently rasterized. What sets this family apart from its siblings is the specific choice of the Gaussian distribution as the primary kernel, characterized by a mean position and a covariance matrix, which allows for analytic integration during volume rendering and efficient gradient propagation.
