 ## Background
-The concept of using Gaussian distributions for scene representation originated with the seminal work on 3D Gaussian Splatting by Kerbl et al. (2023) [G]. Prior to this, neural radiance fields (NeRFs) used continuous MLPs to represent radiance, which were computationally expensive to render. 3D Gaussian Splatting introduced a discrete, explicit representation using Gaussians, enabling real-time rendering. The specific parameterization of the covariance matrix via rotation and scaling was introduced to ensure positive definiteness and efficient optimization. Subsequent variants, such as 2D Gaussian Splatting and Generalized Exponential Splatting, emerged to address limitations in geometry accuracy and high-frequency detail representation, respectively. The LinPrim approach represents an alternative primitive geometry, exploring polyhedral shapes for volumetric rendering [S1]. High-opacity modifications were introduced to improve the modeling of opaque surfaces [S2].
